# Async side effects

After commit, dispatch notification/push/email fan-out through the globally
provided `SideEffectsService.dispatch`; do not await it. Permission-critical work
and content realtime emits remain on the request path; notification/badge emits
belong with their worker-owned writes.

- Declare the name/payload in `SideEffectPayloads`, register it with
  `SideEffectsRegistry` in the owning handler, and add that handler to module
  providers. Drive the handler directly in focused tests.
- Payloads must serialize to JSON. Prefer IDs/primitives and re-read mutable
  content, membership, and access before delivery. Necessary immutable event facts
  may be captured explicitly; do not treat them as current authorization.
- Handlers may retry after partial completion. Use stable event/subject keys and
  the existing notification upsert, push coalescing, and email delivery seams;
  queue job IDs alone are not permanent deduplication.
- Bound recipient work with `runInBatches` from `batch.ts`; large notification
  fan-outs use the existing `notification.fanout.chunk` path and chunk constants.
  Inspect returned failures; batching settles errors rather than throwing them.
- `dispatch` falls back to an in-process handler when enqueue fails. That fallback
  has no retry/durability; enqueue-after-commit also has a process-death gap.
- Production `setImmediate` is confined to this fallback. Notification write/push
  capabilities stay off request paths except documented exceptions in
  `side-effects-guardrails.spec.ts`; read/read-state capabilities are separate.

Implementation and guardrails: `src/modules/side-effects/side-effects.service.ts`,
`side-effects.constants.ts`, `side-effects.registry.ts`, `batch.ts`, and
`side-effects-guardrails.spec.ts`. Queue health: `GET /admin/jobs/queues`.
